#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "7d93dd10-5319-58e7-9106-f8c675dc9e65" :question/math-academy-id "e-12567" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42109de0-eb59-53f9-982e-1433f630b067" :question/math-academy-id "e-12568" :question/problem "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "26c3579b-5b24-5e84-9024-5b2597856d20" :question/math-academy-id "e-12569" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e8c24f26-7658-5251-922a-4b34043a71d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12580" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deb6fb4f-5339-5b74-9add-c3e30549f18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-8\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327997} {:answer/id #uuid "0192fd77-cb66-5e5a-b5ed-0c1404e58d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327998} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c437dc-97eb-5a5a-a08b-e5fd4a6aa48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327999} {:answer/id #uuid "9bda32d7-5faa-50e9-ba02-91217dfb9842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328000} {:answer/id #uuid "76def20f-207d-5d53-8045-fe6bb5fbcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76def20f-207d-5d53-8045-fe6bb5fbcc07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f384411b-3df5-543a-ac7f-98e30d61a666" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0ecbb89-df3b-51f8-acb9-98f79c6ce59a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126739" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ (3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k & =2\\,i+j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3t+2=2 \\\\ 6t+1=1 \\\\ 1-4{t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8(0)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,1,1)$ is $3\\,i+6\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed776e7-5556-5e07-9003-cc39c86c5882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i+j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384789} {:answer/id #uuid "be93162f-bfae-5e1c-a834-119877a93ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384790} {:answer/id #uuid "20f1048b-362a-5a39-8ad5-464b7454e370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384791} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc04868-9c6c-5c3c-b5d1-efa9696ef37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384792} {:answer/id #uuid "c9002579-9caa-5784-898d-3127cede01ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20f1048b-362a-5a39-8ad5-464b7454e370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384791} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a53b87b-f96e-5fa7-96f0-b4c84d3a262d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8009a1f3-562e-594d-a545-8fc2a763c667" :question/math-academy-id "q-126740" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ba406b-6a54-5e5f-9d0e-ab0e678b60b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384796} {:answer/id #uuid "3a292bbd-4d76-56fe-b817-f7d66b5a15d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384797} {:answer/id #uuid "0403453c-64c0-5ce9-9966-4f7e5075b722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384798} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9925e5-528e-5c60-9b74-58964a374a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384799} {:answer/id #uuid "689d3fde-12a9-5d8a-8153-f3790191ec69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e9925e5-528e-5c60-9b74-58964a374a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384799} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e682eed-025e-57b8-bba1-1fd22fe69413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "844f2cf4-dad9-5ec2-9ea0-6093c575cda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126741" :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-1,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=-2,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(-2)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(-2) & =⟨3(-2)^{2},2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=-2$ is $⟨12,-4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd9cf26-36a3-573f-be14-4292f29ef821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347755} {:answer/id #uuid "19c9af6e-fd42-5fe8-a8a0-4ef5c76c9b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347756} {:answer/id #uuid "d79405e0-cf4f-56f0-ba10-aaf9cddf88c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347757} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3f6f05-e2b9-5329-9364-d0192d174f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347758} {:answer/id #uuid "0d66860a-50b2-5812-a611-399d148a0e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c9af6e-fd42-5fe8-a8a0-4ef5c76c9b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347756} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfba4061-161c-5e71-9c8e-3ddfb8354902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28b07468-67e6-5160-b480-58f04dacf3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126835" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da597414-513f-5bd6-a970-4468bc79f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328004} {:answer/id #uuid "2c757345-f3f3-5125-ab6e-f2e00fbbcf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328005} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8bd160-4b5d-5a9b-a2fe-be7939a0f27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328006} {:answer/id #uuid "930d540f-d415-5223-b285-f6ced5ef39a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328007} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9091cf-5dfd-5a21-9a8d-08e15352a916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da597414-513f-5bd6-a970-4468bc79f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328004} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04997be-fe9b-53ad-ae93-7a7906d1c3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b063a87b-7453-5eed-82c3-d4c7e3cbe51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126838" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d9dcebc-a139-5007-958b-97ed08846351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384803} {:answer/id #uuid "13f5b458-a180-5535-9f7f-c4ada5686a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384804} {:answer/id #uuid "0adf7243-5284-54dc-ac2a-7cdf41c999de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384805} {:answer/id #uuid "997ac8b5-6d4e-592d-8a51-0f733e0b4431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384806} {:answer/id #uuid "45d3646f-b622-5296-a354-6bead82b247b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0adf7243-5284-54dc-ac2a-7cdf41c999de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384805} :answer-field/id #uuid "b591f002-5937-58f1-80d8-406981dae9b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1276b48a-33df-5447-9033-1c8e5ee23a8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126841" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f17078fb-8914-5ae5-8ae5-195fe58c2fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384810} {:answer/id #uuid "543e7f42-add6-5143-a68b-7f5b85341cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,3)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384811} {:answer/id #uuid "844fe467-938a-5bca-b016-7b00de5a40b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384812} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc12d06-135c-544b-bf12-7c33bdc759e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384813} {:answer/id #uuid "97c1913a-ba30-5a77-8697-39763768d01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c1913a-ba30-5a77-8697-39763768d01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384814} :answer-field/id #uuid "0745e2b9-9746-505d-aeca-78fa5d05001c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3036af0f-cdd6-5719-9fb5-7af4d1ad21ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-126898" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t+2,-2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0(2t+2)+(-1)(-2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{2}+2(0),4-{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92caf63e-00f9-5f84-b79a-fe883d689ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384817} {:answer/id #uuid "a51b9d93-8c9b-5f79-a6ac-f845f0eeebf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-3)$ and $(10,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384818} {:answer/id #uuid "2f04db5c-fd40-56d1-a58f-f9ad41af947f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384819} {:answer/id #uuid "8be9b795-3a22-5cfe-ac02-27e2067f23bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384820} {:answer/id #uuid "67c39988-8e24-5ffc-979e-88c35e1afd86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ and $(5,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f04db5c-fd40-56d1-a58f-f9ad41af947f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384819} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af94585-d64c-5d88-81ed-04a13acccc98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff607ad4-3a0c-567a-af90-b6d86c0c0c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-164834" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2t-1⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 2t+(-2)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2t-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{(2)}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2(2)-1⟩=⟨5\\,,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(5,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc6e9db-849c-5bd8-b604-b1c473af7d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384824} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd80cfa-2b81-5b0f-b8a8-6555a8569952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384825} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3db90a-3990-5cdc-8b14-b09ac51f7cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,\\sqrt{5})$ and $(1,\\sqrt{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384826} {:answer/id #uuid "3024898d-8ae8-575f-88f0-b04db53cea97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384827} {:answer/id #uuid "278e0aa6-8711-5981-a4ed-45e953863c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc6e9db-849c-5bd8-b604-b1c473af7d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384824} :answer-field/id #uuid "06538711-602d-56fb-918a-9be8c8da82f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e8e0518-1ce8-516d-ae9f-e29a7b523b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164838" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j]\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (t+2)\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}} & =0 \\\\ t+2+t & =0 \\\\ 2t+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1)=(-1+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c0aa963-0a03-5c71-a92c-d39d53224e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,-10)$ and $(-1,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384831} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6ef4e7-e098-539e-814c-4ab54f6240c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384833} {:answer/id #uuid "f3017cf7-4fdd-53c0-9a64-f67616761c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384834} {:answer/id #uuid "4e647582-3df9-549a-a678-3e528cbee9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a862fa-4bce-59e3-8814-cae185814e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d162fc-fa25-54c2-902f-d78114c9c4d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47616907-68ba-5ff3-ae24-1153f4ea3fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-165040" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28300222-2152-58d1-b114-0b72c89c4d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24,4,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384838} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a7fcfd-b066-5b70-b592-fd63141e2060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,-1,-1)$ and $(-3,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384839} {:answer/id #uuid "b701276d-f296-54ff-9fa2-9780efd1a5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384840} {:answer/id #uuid "55d4252a-71d1-55fa-98ce-d97d570c0130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-3,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384841} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e81dca-1177-58d9-bbea-1f81c20fd255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b701276d-f296-54ff-9fa2-9780efd1a5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384840} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48e8efa-e08f-592d-91ee-3669185f395e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a796aed-90b9-5717-b027-1443eb67fe70" :question/math-academy-id "q-165041" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}+4t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-2t\\,,\\,2{t}^{3}-3t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}+4=0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2=c \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-3=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=-1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=1.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{4}+4(-1)\\,,\\,(-1)^{3}-2(-1)\\,,\\,2(-1)^{3}-3(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-3,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022635c3-365e-5ba4-a622-5920d444af77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328046} {:answer/id #uuid "99163740-bc18-5635-82e0-62e10dd759e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328047} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbe3d5d-1a19-5ebb-abf7-82056512a1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328048} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf53f94-0d92-566e-84df-1651262caefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328049} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4ad1d7-ed77-5726-b826-8aa9a21071b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf53f94-0d92-566e-84df-1651262caefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328049} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd84109-53ed-50bc-9c91-d849ab549cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b31a7b97-1eea-55d0-bd37-511197dc3f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-165042" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c43b6daf-aa85-5bba-98c1-47b6a22b594a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117391} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88588-ef76-5b00-bb1d-b137c461ad7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117392} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a1398-aed1-5a44-8ab3-a2e31fb96c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117393} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ab2de-0954-547c-930d-34fd15fd5a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b5b6cab-7226-55ef-bdaf-b517cc71e5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117394} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa839e10-db01-577e-91c1-f151d47846a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e23a960-2958-5b70-bc1c-e4672cc3a4bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32916" :question/problem "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9103c5ba-b2e6-581d-98c7-417728faea75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+4\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384845} {:answer/id #uuid "54849260-7bda-5b12-a045-533610d05541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384846} {:answer/id #uuid "72fc43ab-fcb6-5277-887c-9bc3bcd859cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+\\,j+4\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384847} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ba7345-6c92-5211-8914-ce3161ee5706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+4\\,j+5\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384848} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdafb7e-9ecc-52e6-9c20-1bf74f5191ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54849260-7bda-5b12-a045-533610d05541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384846} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a94534-0f8c-59e9-9763-193ad0be1c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4b42052-49fd-542c-8c2e-de0d2cea6678" :question/math-academy-id "q-33450" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k$ at the point $P(1,2,1),$ \nvalid for real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+k \\\\ t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {t}^{5}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+5{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+5(1)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316ae081-928b-5565-9dbf-30b4dcd09ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328074} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f107f4-57b7-5477-90f2-8b1c92eab0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328075} {:answer/id #uuid "0b309581-2f7e-5146-968b-f0aed58c91e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328076} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b459bd-b7e0-5d77-a96e-5b6e03a657fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328077} {:answer/id #uuid "738fc50c-2493-53cb-b0ff-e857dba80d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738fc50c-2493-53cb-b0ff-e857dba80d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328078} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f12857-a212-588b-8b4b-db5fc9d02ad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da0d02a-cfac-5869-8101-5b8d71d9f0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33461" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef6dafa-2e60-5e9b-a177-67ae78d626e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$ and $(2, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117637} {:answer/id #uuid "0444b013-203c-597d-8d1a-207ab2748493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117638} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc1336b-4154-589f-aeb7-536301c7858a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1)$, $(2, 2)$, and $(-2, 2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117639} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4ca137-a16c-53c6-bff5-cb55721644c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 3)$ and $(-2, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117640} {:answer/id #uuid "20a58f35-b575-517b-99e2-921560f0176f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20a58f35-b575-517b-99e2-921560f0176f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117641} :answer-field/id #uuid "045680ab-260e-513f-9d4c-2dcae7ac41b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8969d65-ef55-525e-9adb-a40d8a396669" :question/math-academy-id "q-33498" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4092311-d1b7-5fa7-b81f-0952d37a7097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3,2)$ and $(-1,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328088} {:answer/id #uuid "b024fe5f-5064-5c4d-84f4-28d34e27453f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,10,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328089} {:answer/id #uuid "3a1c4f15-f41d-5503-920d-a11c8eba1348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328090} {:answer/id #uuid "90d85cb5-751b-5d46-8738-f5b4f57ae23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328091} {:answer/id #uuid "99f37592-7eba-5993-9be1-2bc6083a52ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,1)$ and $(-1,1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d85cb5-751b-5d46-8738-f5b4f57ae23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328091} :answer-field/id #uuid "206d5385-55fb-55ed-93f0-a1927392cc00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd4dff7b-a812-5fe4-af3d-a9ec36938cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-33509" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36da28d4-f387-5b56-ae74-50ef6df04c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384852} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2c0dbd-2398-57f9-bb3f-2409989f15f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384853} {:answer/id #uuid "66d5ee8f-08e7-5ba0-8fd8-c82ac8e24ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1,{e}^{-1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384854} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0226d9-f7dc-5430-9f2d-cefe2e9aeaa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384855} {:answer/id #uuid "b998a898-0fe8-566b-a557-c1c9680162c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,0)$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f0226d9-f7dc-5430-9f2d-cefe2e9aeaa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384855} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae24cfe-765f-51e1-847b-119c759a0542" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03c1928d-c4a6-5eab-96f2-0308ab26f5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33517" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1}=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c2fbd5-33cf-51fb-b95d-cf5277f3e4ae" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "915ba534-c3c8-5855-9647-e725fd4d2a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384859} {:answer/id #uuid "4d52db5d-2a40-50ac-9cf7-f1e6b48157bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384860} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb7a9c6-05bb-5210-8de1-de0c591b9d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384861} {:answer/id #uuid "c82ac96a-19b6-51f7-94ce-09d73692248f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384862} {:answer/id #uuid "e457188c-7b7c-5a67-b379-68b812c85d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fb7a9c6-05bb-5210-8de1-de0c591b9d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384861} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d574db0-5415-542a-905a-59b56e605cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c71f4268-3db5-5cce-9d26-514d27b4fd1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75199" :question/problem "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0},\\,2\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is $⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a1cf6b7-c4db-5e2a-ab3b-bdae218f32af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-7)$ and $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384866} {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c001be-d3ba-5db9-88c3-50ac6855ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384868} {:answer/id #uuid "d5825a57-dd67-512c-a791-252eebf2d7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384869} {:answer/id #uuid "f451166f-d800-5ebe-be3f-3f43a56f6409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c276f960-8bb5-5ebf-b5fe-cfaeeab8cbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384867} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e6bf08-2a63-5c42-a511-7482134d283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a15bf605-19f6-54de-9516-f3cb67a15bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75205" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+1⟩,t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+3t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$, the only solution is $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =⟨{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}+1⟩=⟨\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}).$"}]}
